---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { site } from '../../../data/site';

const title = 'Full PDR Workmanship Warranty Terms | Peak Country';
const description = 'Read the complete Limited Lifetime PDR Workmanship Warranty terms for repairs performed by Peak Country Auto Hail Repair & Paintless Dent Repair.';
---
<Layout title={title} description={description} exactTitle>
  <header class="page-head standards-head">
    <div class="eyebrow">Clear terms · Careful repair</div>
    <h1>Peak Country Limited Lifetime Workmanship Warranty<br />Full Warranty Terms</h1>
    <p>Complete terms for qualifying paintless dent repair workmanship performed by Peak Country.</p>
  </header>

  <article class="section narrow prose warranty-terms">
    <h2>Our Workmanship Commitment</h2>
    <p>Peak Country Auto Hail Repair &amp; Paintless Dent Repair stands behind the workmanship it performs.</p>
    <p>Paintless dent repairs completed by Peak Country are covered by this Limited Lifetime Workmanship Warranty for as long as the original customer owns the repaired vehicle.</p>
    <p>If a covered repair develops a problem that is determined to be the result of Peak Country’s workmanship, Peak Country will inspect the repair and correct the covered workmanship issue at no charge to the customer.</p>
    <p>This warranty applies only to the specific repair work performed by Peak Country and is subject to the terms and exclusions below.</p>

    <h2>1. Warranty Period</h2>
    <p>This workmanship warranty begins on the date the covered repair is completed.</p>
    <p>Coverage continues for as long as the original customer who authorized the repair owns the vehicle.</p>
    <p>The warranty ends when ownership of the vehicle is sold, traded, donated, transferred, or otherwise changes from the original customer.</p>
    <p>This warranty is non-transferable.</p>

    <h2>2. Covered Paintless Dent Repair Workmanship</h2>
    <p>This warranty applies to workmanship associated with paintless dent repair performed directly by Peak Country.</p>
    <p>Covered workmanship may include, when applicable:</p>
    <ul><li>Correction of the specific dent or hail damage included in the authorized repair</li><li>Work performed during the PDR process</li><li>Removal and reinstallation workmanship performed by Peak Country as part of the repair</li><li>Reassembly workmanship associated with the repair</li><li>Other workmanship specifically documented as part of the completed repair</li></ul>
    <p>Coverage applies only when the concern is directly related to workmanship performed by Peak Country.</p>

    <h2>3. PDR Access, Leverage and Normal Access Conditions</h2>
    <p>Paintless dent repair commonly requires specialized tools to reach the backside of damaged panels.</p>
    <p>Depending on the vehicle and location of the damage, access may involve:</p>
    <ul><li>Existing factory openings</li><li>Drain or service openings</li><li>Openings behind trim or interior components</li><li>Access through braces or structural cavities</li><li>Removal of components</li><li>Creation of a small access opening when reasonably necessary to perform the repair</li></ul>
    <p>The use of an appropriate access opening is a normal PDR technique when direct access is otherwise limited and the method is appropriate for the vehicle and repair.</p>
    <p>PDR tools depend on controlled leverage and pressure. As a result, minor tool-contact marks or minor localized distortion may occur around concealed access points or openings during normal repair operations.</p>
    <p>Minor access-related conditions are not considered a workmanship defect when they:</p>
    <ul><li>Are limited to a concealed or non-cosmetic area</li><li>Do not materially affect the exterior appearance of the vehicle</li><li>Do not interfere with the fit or function of the affected component</li><li>Do not compromise vehicle safety or structural function</li><li>Do not unnecessarily damage surrounding wiring, electronics, hoses, lines, or other vehicle systems</li></ul>
    <p>Any access opening created during the repair will be kept reasonably limited to what is necessary for the repair and treated appropriately after use.</p>
    <p>This provision does not excuse excessive, unnecessary, or careless damage caused during the repair.</p>

    <h2>4. Corrosion Protection</h2>
    <p>Paintless dent repair and access procedures can disturb factory corrosion protection, coatings, seam protection, or expose bare metal in concealed areas of a vehicle.</p>
    <p>When corrosion protection is affected by the repair, Peak Country will make reasonable, good-faith efforts to restore protection to accessible areas using professional products and application methods consistent with recognized OEM corrosion-protection and collision-repair practices.</p>
    <p>Depending on the repair and vehicle construction, this may include the appropriate use of:</p>
    <ul><li>E-coat or equivalent corrosion-resistant coatings</li><li>Cavity wax</li><li>Corrosion-protection coatings</li><li>Sealants</li><li>Access-hole plugs</li><li>Other suitable corrosion-prevention materials or methods</li></ul>
    <p>The specific materials and application method used will depend on the location of the repair, vehicle construction, accessibility, and the type of factory protection affected.</p>
    <p>Where an access opening is created, Peak Country will take reasonable steps to protect exposed metal and finish the opening using an appropriate corrosion-protection method and plug or closure when applicable.</p>
    <p>Vehicle panels frequently contain enclosed cavities, internal braces, reinforcements, and areas that cannot be completely accessed after assembly. These physical limitations may prevent corrosion-protection products from reaching every internal surface or reproducing the exact coverage applied during original vehicle manufacturing.</p>
    <p>For that reason, Peak Country does not represent that corrosion protection performed during a PDR repair provides absolute or permanent protection against future corrosion.</p>
    <p>Peak Country’s obligation is to use reasonable professional judgment and make a good-faith effort to protect accessible areas affected by the repair using appropriate materials and application methods.</p>
    <p>Future corrosion is not considered a workmanship defect solely because corrosion protection could not completely reach an inaccessible internal surface.</p>
    <p>This limitation does not excuse a failure to reasonably protect an accessible area when corrosion protection is appropriate as part of the repair.</p>

    <h2>5. Removal and Reinstallation Workmanship</h2>
    <p>When components must be removed and reinstalled to access a repair area, Peak Country warrants the workmanship involved in that removal and reinstallation.</p>
    <p>This may include applicable:</p>
    <ul><li>Trim</li><li>Interior components</li><li>Lamps</li><li>Liners</li><li>Moldings</li><li>Panels</li><li>Shields</li><li>Similar components removed as part of the repair</li></ul>
    <p>The warranty applies to Peak Country’s removal, installation, and reassembly workmanship.</p>
    <p>It does not create a lifetime warranty on the component itself.</p>

    <h2>6. Parts, Clips, Adhesives and Components</h2>
    <p>Replacement parts and materials may be necessary during some repairs.</p>
    <p>These may include:</p>
    <ul><li>Clips</li><li>Fasteners</li><li>Retainers</li><li>Moldings</li><li>Adhesives</li><li>Trim components</li><li>Electrical components</li><li>Sensors</li><li>Lamps</li><li>Manufacturer components</li><li>Other replacement parts or materials</li></ul>
    <p>These items are not covered as parts by Peak Country’s lifetime workmanship warranty.</p>
    <p>Any warranty applicable to a replacement component may instead be provided by the manufacturer, supplier, or distributor of that component.</p>
    <p>Peak Country’s workmanship associated with installing an applicable replacement component remains subject to this warranty.</p>

    <h2>7. What This Warranty Does Not Cover</h2>
    <p>This warranty does not cover damage, deterioration, failure, or changes unrelated to Peak Country’s workmanship.</p>
    <p>Examples include, but are not limited to:</p>
    <ul><li>New dents or damage occurring after the repair</li><li>Subsequent hail damage</li><li>Collision damage</li><li>Door impacts</li><li>Shopping-cart damage</li><li>Road debris</li><li>Vandalism</li><li>Environmental damage</li><li>Corrosion or rust unrelated to Peak Country’s workmanship</li><li>Corrosion occurring in inaccessible internal areas despite reasonable corrosion-protection efforts</li><li>Normal wear and aging</li><li>Paint deterioration unrelated to the repair</li><li>Factory paint or material defects</li><li>Pre-existing damage</li><li>Pre-existing paint damage</li><li>Previous repairs performed by another repairer</li><li>Previous body filler, refinishing, or structural repair</li><li>Hidden conditions that could not reasonably be identified before or during the repair</li><li>Failure of an original vehicle component</li><li>Failure of a replacement component</li><li>Manufacturer defects or recalls</li><li>Electronic or mechanical failures unrelated to Peak Country’s workmanship</li><li>Damage resulting from later modification, alteration, disassembly, repair, or refinishing of the repaired area</li><li>Damage caused by another repair facility, technician, vehicle owner, installer, or third party after Peak Country’s repair</li><li>Damage resulting from misuse, neglect, improper maintenance, or abnormal vehicle use</li><li>Normal minor access conditions described in Section 3</li></ul>

    <h2>8. Paint and Pre-Existing Repair Conditions</h2>
    <p>Paintless dent repair is intended to preserve the vehicle’s existing finish whenever reasonably possible.</p>
    <p>Peak Country cannot provide a lifetime warranty on:</p>
    <ul><li>Factory paint</li><li>Previously refinished paint</li><li>Body filler</li><li>Previous repairs</li><li>Corrosion</li><li>Existing material deterioration</li><li>Other pre-existing conditions</li></ul>
    <p>Previously repaired or refinished areas may react differently during PDR than undamaged original factory materials.</p>
    <p>When Peak Country identifies a condition that may materially affect the repair, the customer will be informed when reasonably practical.</p>

    <h2>9. Subsequent Damage to a Previously Repaired Area</h2>
    <p>A panel previously repaired by Peak Country may later sustain new damage.</p>
    <p>New damage does not automatically indicate failure of the original repair and is not covered by this warranty.</p>
    <p>If there is uncertainty about whether a condition is related to Peak Country’s original workmanship or subsequent damage, Peak Country will inspect the vehicle and evaluate the concern.</p>

    <h2>10. Reporting a Warranty Concern</h2>
    <p>A customer who discovers a condition believed to be related to Peak Country’s workmanship must notify Peak Country within 30 calendar days after discovering the condition, or within 30 calendar days after the condition reasonably should have been discovered.</p>
    <p>The purpose of this requirement is to provide Peak Country a reasonable opportunity to inspect the condition before it changes, deteriorates, is altered, or becomes difficult to evaluate.</p>
    <p>Failure to provide timely notice may affect warranty coverage when the delay materially interferes with Peak Country’s ability to determine the cause of the condition or reasonably correct the covered workmanship issue.</p>
    <p>Applicable law may provide additional rights that are not limited by this provision.</p>

    <h2>11. Warranty Inspection</h2>
    <p>Customers requesting warranty consideration should provide reasonable information about the concern.</p>
    <p>Peak Country may request:</p>
    <ul><li>Photographs</li><li>Vehicle information</li><li>Original repair documentation</li><li>Approximate repair date</li><li>Description of the concern</li><li>Information regarding subsequent damage or repair</li><li>An opportunity to physically inspect the vehicle</li></ul>
    <p>A physical inspection may be required before warranty coverage can be determined.</p>
    <p>There is no charge for Peak Country’s inspection of a legitimate warranty concern related to its workmanship.</p>

    <h2>12. Warranty Correction</h2>
    <p>If Peak Country determines that a concern is covered by this warranty, Peak Country will correct the covered workmanship issue at no charge to the customer.</p>
    <p>Peak Country will determine the reasonable repair method necessary to correct the covered workmanship concern.</p>
    <p>Warranty coverage is limited to correcting the covered Peak Country workmanship.</p>

    <h2>13. Opportunity to Inspect and Correct</h2>
    <p>Before another repair facility, technician, or third party performs work intended to correct an alleged Peak Country workmanship issue, the customer must provide Peak Country a reasonable opportunity to inspect the concern and, if covered, correct it.</p>
    <p>If another party alters, repairs, refinishes, disassembles, or modifies the affected area before Peak Country has a reasonable opportunity to inspect it, Peak Country may be unable to determine the cause of the condition.</p>
    <p>Interference with Peak Country’s ability to inspect or determine the cause may limit or prevent warranty coverage for that concern.</p>
    <p>Emergency action reasonably necessary to protect the vehicle or prevent additional damage is not prohibited by this provision.</p>

    <h2>14. Customer Responsibilities</h2>
    <p>To obtain warranty consideration, the customer should:</p>
    <ul><li>Notify Peak Country within the reporting period described in this warranty</li><li>Provide accurate information about the vehicle and original repair</li><li>Disclose subsequent damage or work involving the affected area</li><li>Avoid unnecessary alteration of the affected area before inspection</li><li>Make the vehicle reasonably available for inspection</li><li>Allow Peak Country a reasonable opportunity to correct a covered workmanship concern</li></ul>

    <h2>15. Consequential and Incidental Costs</h2>
    <p>This warranty provides for the inspection and correction of covered Peak Country workmanship.</p>
    <p>Because Peak Country primarily performs cosmetic vehicle repairs, this workmanship warranty does not include reimbursement or payment for incidental, indirect, or consequential expenses associated with a warranty concern.</p>
    <p>Examples include, but are not limited to:</p>
    <ul><li>Rental vehicle expenses</li><li>Towing</li><li>Transportation expenses</li><li>Fuel</li><li>Lodging</li><li>Travel expenses</li><li>Lost wages</li><li>Lost income</li><li>Business interruption</li><li>Loss of vehicle use</li><li>Inconvenience</li><li>Diminished value</li><li>Other indirect or consequential costs</li></ul>
    <p>Peak Country’s obligation under this warranty is limited to inspection and correction of the covered workmanship issue itself, to the extent permitted by applicable law.</p>
    <p>Nothing in this section limits any right or remedy that cannot legally be limited under applicable law.</p>

    <h2>16. Insurance Repairs</h2>
    <p>This workmanship warranty applies to qualifying repairs regardless of whether the customer paid directly or the repair was associated with an insurance claim.</p>
    <p>Insurance involvement does not expand or reduce Peak Country’s workmanship obligations under this warranty.</p>
    <p>Insurance coverage decisions, policy benefits, claim approvals, deductibles, insurer payments, and other insurance matters are separate from Peak Country’s workmanship warranty and are governed by the applicable insurance policy and insurer.</p>

    <h2>17. Scope of Warranty</h2>
    <p>This warranty applies only to workmanship performed by Peak Country.</p>
    <p>It does not represent a warranty from:</p>
    <ul><li>A vehicle manufacturer</li><li>An insurance company</li><li>A parts manufacturer</li><li>A parts supplier</li><li>Another repair facility</li><li>Any other third party</li></ul>
    <p>Nothing in this warranty should be interpreted as representing Peak Country as affiliated with or endorsed by a vehicle manufacturer or insurance company unless such an affiliation is separately documented.</p>

    <h2>18. Warranty Records</h2>
    <p>Peak Country may retain:</p>
    <ul><li>Repair records</li><li>Photographs</li><li>Estimates</li><li>Invoices</li><li>Authorizations</li><li>Damage documentation</li><li>Other records associated with the repair</li></ul>
    <p>Customers are encouraged to retain their final invoice and repair documentation.</p>
    <p>Reasonable proof of the original covered repair may be required when requesting warranty service.</p>

    <div class="about-actions">
      <a class="button" href="/warranty/">View Warranty Summary</a>
      <a class="text-link" href={`tel:${site.phone}`}>Call {site.phoneDisplay} with a warranty question →</a>
    </div>
    <p class="warranty-pdf-note">The approved downloadable warranty PDF is available upon request. A download link will be added here when the approved file is supplied.</p>
  </article>
</Layout>
